Q1) Java 21 Virtual thread ?

Ans)

What is a Virtual Thread?
  A lightweight thread managed by the JVM, not directly tied to an OS thread. 
  It lets applications handle large numbers of concurrent tasks, especially I/O-heavy ones.

Why were they introduced?
  Platform threads are expensive because each is backed by an OS thread. 
  Virtual threads make thousands or even millions of concurrent tasks practical.

Faster than platform threads?
  Not necessarily. The advantage is higher concurrency and scalability, not faster individual operations.
  Note: Virtual threads improve concurrency, not CPU performance.

PLATFORM VS VIRTUAL THREADS
---------------------------
  Platform Thread
    - Backed by an OS thread
    - More expensive
    - Uses more memory
    - Limited number can be created
    - Suitable for CPU-intensive work
  Virtual Thread
    - Managed by the JVM
    - Extremely lightweight
    - Can have very large numbers
    - Excellent for I/O-bound workloads
    - Designed for high concurrency

  Can we create millions of Virtual Threads?
    Potentially yes, but your database, memory, CPU, network and external APIs still have limits.

  Should they replace thread pools?
    Not completely. For many I/O tasks, use one virtual thread per task instead of a small worker pool.

HOW VIRTUAL THREADS WORK
------------------------
  What is a Carrier Thread?
    A platform thread that temporarily executes a virtual thread. Many virtual threads share a smaller number of carrier threads.
  
  What happens when a Virtual Thread blocks?
    Step 1. Virtual Thread runs on Carrier
    Step 2. Blocking I/O
    Step 3. Virtual Thread unmounts
    Step 4. Carrier becomes available
    Step 5. Another Virtual Thread runs
            When the I/O finishes, the virtual thread continues.
  
  Does every Virtual Thread have an OS thread?
    No. Thousands are multiplexed over far fewer carrier threads, so 10,000 waiting requests don't need 10,000 OS threads.

I/O-BOUND VS CPU-BOUND
----------------------
  Why are they good for I/O?
    I/O mostly waits (HTTP, database, file, network, socket operations). While one virtual thread waits, the carrier runs another task.
  
  Good for CPU-heavy tasks?
    Not particularly. More virtual threads don't create more CPU cores.
    Example
    Good:
    var result = httpClient.send(request);
    Not automatically better:
    for (long i = 0; i < 1_000_000_000; i++) {
        calculate(i);
    }
  
  INTERVIEW RULE
    - I/O-bound → Virtual Threads shine
    - CPU-bound → Limit concurrency according to available CPU

PINNING & SYNCHRONIZATION
-------------------------
  What is Virtual Thread Pinning?
    A virtual thread cannot easily unmount from its carrier during a blocking operation, which reduces scalability.
  
  Can synchronized cause pinning?
    Yes, when blocking while holding a monitor:
    synchronized (lock) {
        slowBlockingCall();
    }
  
  Better approach: keep synchronized sections short
  synchronized (lock) {
      updateState();
  }
  slowBlockingCall();
  
  
  Is synchronized forbidden?
    No. The problem is long blocking work while holding a monitor, not synchronized itself.

DATABASES, RESOURCES & SPRING BOOT
----------------------------------
  Do they remove database limits?
    No. Virtual threads are cheap. Database connections are not.
    10,000 Virtual Threads → 100 DB Connections → Database is still the bottleneck.
  
  Can they overload an API?
    Yes. Higher concurrency can overwhelm databases, REST APIs, third-party services, connection pools and memory.
    Use rate limits, semaphores, connection pools and timeouts.
  
  Can Spring Boot use them?
    Yes, on a suitable Java version:
    spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true
  
  INTERVIEW LINE
  Virtual threads remove thread scarcity, not system-resource limits.

CREATING VIRTUAL THREADS
-------------------------
  How do you create a Virtual Thread?
    Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    });
    Or:
    Thread.ofVirtual()
          .start(() -> doWork());
  
  How do you run many independent tasks?
    try (var executor =
            Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
            executor.submit(() -> callApi());
        }
    }

PRACTICAL QUESTIONS
-------------------
  A fixed pool of Virtual Threads?
    Usually no. Create one virtual thread per task and limit the actual scarce resource instead.
  
  What if every task accesses a database?
    10,000 virtual threads doesn't mean 10,000 simultaneous queries. Control access with the connection pool or 
    another concurrency mechanism.
  
  What if a task throws an exception?
    With submit(), it is retrieved through the returned Future:
    Future<?> future = executor.submit(() -> {
        throw new RuntimeException("Failed");
    });
    
    future.get();
  
  Do they improve latency?
    Not automatically. They improve throughput and scalability, but don't make a database or network request faster.




